<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Clemens came in without an appointment with a cough for six days after a head cold, now bringing up a little white sputum, with a temperature up to 37.6 °C and tiredness. He has no shortness of breath at rest, no chest pain and no blood in the sputum; the chest tightness he had on hills has not returned. He has taken varenicline for two weeks, now 1 mg twice daily, with mild nausea after breakfast, and is down to four cigars a day; his quit date is tomorrow. He asked whether he should put it off until the cough is better, and whether he should take a cold remedy with an antihistamine and decongestant that a friend recommended. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Six days ago, after a cold</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Chest</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Six days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Cough with a little white sputum</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; wakes him at night</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Worse in the morning and at night</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Cigar smoker cutting down on varenicline; quit date tomorrow; coronary disease on a statin and aspirin</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Honey and hot drinks help; worse after a cigar</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Low-grade temperature and tiredness; no shortness of breath, chest pain or blood in the sputum</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

