<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute bronchitis, likely viral (J20.9)</content>. His pulse is under 100, his respiratory rate under 24, his temperature under 38 °C and his chest shows no sign of consolidation, so pneumonia is unlikely, and a chest X-ray and antibiotics are not needed, in line with ACP and CDC advice. Smoking slows recovery. The cough may last up to three weeks. [S]<list><item>Honey, warm drinks and rest.</item><item>Avoid cold remedies with first-generation antihistamines, which the Beers criteria advise against at his age because of confusion, falls and urinary retention, and with decongestants, which raise blood pressure and heart rate in a man with coronary disease.</item><item>Return for shortness of breath, chest pain, a temperature above 38.5 °C, blood in the sputum, or a cough lasting more than three weeks; spirometry if the cough persists once he is well.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tobacco use disorder on varenicline (F17.290)</content>. The cough is a reason to keep the quit date, not to delay it; most people with a cough after stopping find it clears within weeks. Nausea is mild; taking the tablet with food helps. [S]<list><item>Keep the quit date tomorrow.</item><item>Continue varenicline 1 mg twice daily with food.</item><item>Quitline call this week.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prolonged grief disorder (F43.81)</content>. Grief therapy is continuing. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of death or suicide. [S]<list><item>Continue weekly sessions.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Tobacco Smoking Cessation in Adults, Including Pregnant Persons: Interventions (2021); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016); American Geriatrics Society 2023 Updated AGS Beers Criteria for Potentially Inappropriate Medication Use in Older Adults (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Telephone check in one week about the cough and the quit date. Thirteen-week review in September. Return sooner for any of the warning signs above. Next scheduled visit 1905-09-11: thirteen-week review: grief therapy, smoking, a gout flare while away, the urate and lipid results. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
